        "id": 0,
        "original_id": "5b8930e7-1978-5baf-b049-950a10b572a3",
        "text": "This chunk is about:\n- Plants: Philadelphus, Sarracenia, mock orange\n- Concepts: plant genera, species counts, native ranges, common names\n\nFacts:\n- Philadelphus is a genus of about 60 species of shrubs that grow from 3 to 20 feet (1 to 6 meters) tall.\n- Philadelphus is native to North America, Central America, Asia, and locally to southeast Europe.\n- Sarracenia is a genus of 8 to 11 species of North American pitcher plants.\n- Sarracenia plants are commonly called trumpet pitchers.\n- Mock orange or mock-orange typically refers to Philadelphus, a mostly Holarctic genus of shrubs.\n- Mock orange particularly refers to the species Philadelphus coronarius, which is widely cultivated as an ornamental.",
        "type": "TextSummary"
      },
      {
        "id": 1,
        "original_id": "0fb46ad2-8787-5181-b185-4c4af26d2fba",
        "text": "Page: Philadelphus. Sentence: Philadelphus ( ) (mock-orange) is a genus of about 60 species of shrubs from 3-20 ft (1-6 m) tall, native to North America, Central America, Asia and (locally) in southeast Europe.\nPage: Sarracenia. Sentence: Sarracenia ( or ) is a genus comprising 8 to 11 species of North American pitcher plants, commonly called trumpet pitchers.\nPage: Mock orange. Sentence: Mock orange or mock-orange typically means \"Philadelphus\", a mostly Holarctic genus of shrubs, particularly the species \"Philadelphus coronarius\" which is widely cultivated as an ornamental.",
